<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mangrai</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1238–<a href="/years/1311">1311</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Mengrai, Mangrai the Great</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mangrai was the founder of the northern Thai kingdom of Lan Na. Ruler of Ngoenyang from about 1259, he founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiang-rai">Chiang Rai</a> in 1262, conquered the Mon kingdom of Hariphunchai in 1292 and founded Chiang Mai as his capital in 1296. He allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ngam-mueang">Ngam Mueang</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phayao">Phayao</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ram-khamhaeng">Ram Khamhaeng</a> of Sukhothai, and a law code bears his name.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mangrai.jpg" alt="Mangrai" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Chainwit. · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:King_Mangrai_Statue,_Pho_Khun_Intersection,_Chiang_Rai_(2026)_-_img_03.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
